  "summary": "I reviewed Harold Vance's active medications (pt-0002, MRN 4410-22) against RBC-PHARM-07. Only gabapentin needed a change. I reduced it and left you a progress note (doc-t0002).\n\n**Numbers I used:**\n- **Age:** 82.\n- **Creatinine:** 1.21 mg/dL from 10/20. The 10/21 result of 1.88 mg/dL is marked entered-in-error (mislabelled specimen from another patient), so I didn't use it.\n- **Weight:** 64.9 kg (143 lb), measured 10/20. The 132 lb on 10/22 was his estimate by phone, not a weighing, so I left it out.\n\n**What the policy calls for, drug by drug:**\n- **Apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily: no change.** The policy's apixaban section covers atrial fibrillation only. He has no AF diagnosis; he takes it as long-term prevention after his 2025 pulmonary embolism, per hematology. Their letter says the 2.5 mg dose is \"not a renally adjusted dose.\" Even under the AF rules he would meet only one of three criteria, which would mean 5 mg — the wrong dose for his situation.\n- **Metformin 1000 mg twice daily: no change.** His eGFR is 59.8, and the policy only reduces the dose below 45.\n- **Gabapentin: reduced from 600 mg three times daily (1800 mg/day) to 600 mg twice daily (1200 mg/day).** His creatinine clearance is 43.2 mL/min. For that range the policy allows 400–1400 mg/day, given twice daily. I changed the existing order rather than writing a new one; the replacement is mr-t0001.\n- **Everything else** (atorvastatin, metoprolol, amlodipine, acetaminophen, nitroglycerin spray) is outside the policy and unchanged.\n\nThe pharmacist's 10/21 screen was based on the wrong creatinine, so its suggested changes to apixaban reasoning, metformin and gabapentin don't apply. The note says so.\n\n**Still to do:** nobody has told Mr. Vance about the gabapentin change yet, and someone should keep an eye on his nerve pain at the lower dose. I'd also repeat his creatinine at routine follow-up, given his kidney injury in February.",
